From: Michiel <367462+meghuizen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 14:01:59 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 0/2] fs: skip the struct file allocation for opens that
 fail in the walk

path_openat() allocates the struct file before the path walk and frees
it again when the open fails. Patch 2 allocates it only once an open
needs it, so opens that fail in the walk (ENOENT and friends) no longer
allocate and free one. Patch 1 is an independent selftests Makefile fix
that makes the openat2 tests build again; it can be taken separately.

Based on vfs-7.4.lookup, on top of "fs: avoid spurious dentry ref/unref
cycle on open" (161ce1e692d0), which changes the same do_open() path.
Both patches also apply to v7.3-rc3 on their own.

Measured in an x86-64 guest (nested KVM, 16 vCPUs), patch 2 alone on
v7.3-rc3, 2 boots interleaved with 8 base boots, perf stat in the guest:

  #  impact                          result
  1  low: test coverage              openat2 tests build and pass
  2  medium: opens that fail in the  ENOENT: -22% (ext4), -13% (tmpfs)
     walk (ENOENT)                   kernel instructions per open;
                                     successful open inside the
                                     per-boot spread

The same campaign also measured a dentry reference handoff equivalent to
161ce1e692d0 (dentry only, the mount reference kept): with 16 processes
opening one ext4 file, kernel cycles per open fell 38% (6741 and 7934
against 11326-13011), consistent with the +39% in its changelog. That
patch is not part of this series.

Testing:
- Each commit compiles fs/namei.o and fs/open.o with W=1 (x86-64 config
  with SELinux, BPF LSM, ext4; gcc 15.2), no warnings, on
  vfs-7.4.lookup; patch 2 also on v7.3-rc3.
- checkpatch --strict: clean apart from the missing Signed-off-by.
- Runtime on v7.3-rc3, patch 2 alone and together with the dentry
  handoff, 2 boots each: the openat2 selftests (openat2_test,
  resolve_test, rename_attack_test, emptypath_test); an error-path test
  (O_CREAT|O_TRUNC, O_DIRECT failing after the open, EACCES from
  may_open(), ENOTDIR) followed by umount of the ext4 and tmpfs mounts
  to catch a leaked reference; no kernel warnings.
- Patch 1: the four openat2 programs also build and run on a 6.18 host.

Not tested:
- Runtime on vfs-7.4.lookup itself (only built there).
- Hardware other than the nested KVM guest; wall-clock throughput.
- The file-max limit, network filesystems and ->atomic_open() users,
  filesystems other than ext4 and tmpfs.
- Any architecture other than x86-64; sparse/smatch; allmodconfig.

Tool assistance: the changes, changelogs and measurements in this series
were produced with Claude (Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5) as a coding
assistant, prompted to find and fix performance problems in the VFS and
to measure them. Each patch carries an Assisted-by tag.

